<BaseLayout title="Fuel System" description="In-tank fuel pump upgrades (DW420, DW430C), dedicated fuel pump relay wiring, and EVAP solenoid handling for LS Genesis Coupe swaps.">
  <h1>Fuel System</h1>

  <h2 id="pump">In-Tank Pump Options</h2>
  <p>An LS needs more fuel than the stock Genesis engines, so many builders upgrade the in-tank pump while the tank is open. Drop-in options listed for the Genesis Coupe:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Pump</th><th>Flow</th><th>Fits</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>DeatschWerks DW420 (with install kit)</td><td>420 lph</td><td>2010–12 Genesis Coupe (2.0T and 3.8)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>DeatschWerks DW430C compact</td><td>430 lph</td><td>2013–16 3.8, 2013–14 2.0T</td></tr>
      <tr><td>DeatschWerks DW300 series</td><td>340 lph</td><td>Genesis Coupe 2.0T / 3.8 (check year)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Fitment listings vary between sellers, so confirm your exact year and trim. Fuel pumps usually aren't returnable.</p>
  <p>On the engine side, Sikky sells a Genesis fuel line kit (FFK-GEN, $55.99) to connect the factory feed line to the LS rail.</p>

  <h2 id="surge-tank">Surge Tank Setup (High HP)</h2>
  <p>
    For bigger power, especially with boost, some builds add a surge tank with its own pumps instead of relying on one in-tank pump.
    The surge tank stays full, so the pumps feeding the engine never suck air in hard corners or on a low tank.
  </p>
  <h3 id="surge-tank-how">How the fuel flows</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>A low-pressure <strong>lift pump</strong> in the factory tank keeps the surge tank topped up.</li>
    <li>The surge tank's own high-pressure pumps feed the fuel rail.</li>
    <li>The fuel pressure regulator returns extra fuel to the <strong>surge tank</strong>.</li>
    <li>When the surge tank is full, the overflow line sends fuel back to the main tank.</li>
  </ol>
