  <h3 id="surge-tank-example">Example: Osiris surge tank with two Walbro 525s</h3>
  <p>
    This build uses an Osiris surge tank mounted in the trunk with <strong>two Walbro 525 pumps</strong>, a fuel pressure gauge on top, a relay for each pump,
    and AN lines run through bulkhead fittings in the floor.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Buy genuine Walbro 525 pumps with the built-in check valve:</strong> part <strong>F90000285</strong>
      (<a href="https://www.racetronix.biz/p/walbro-525-fuel-pump-f90000285-e85-hellcat/f90000285" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Racetronix listing</a>).
      In a dual-pump setup the check valve keeps one pump from pushing fuel back through the other, and it holds prime after shutdown.
      Racetronix showed it out of stock in November 2026, so you may need another authorized Walbro/TI dealer. Racetronix rates it at about 465 LPH at 43.5 psi and 429 LPH at 58 psi, and it is E85-rated.
      Each pump draws roughly 17–19 A, so give each one its own relay and heavy enough wire.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="figs">
    <Figure src="surge-tank-dual-walbro-525-hanger.jpg" alt="Surge tank pump hanger with two Walbro 525 fuel pumps, filter socks and wiring, FP1 labeled" caption="Pump hanger with two Walbro 525s (FP1 marked)." />
    <Figure src="osiris-surge-tank-trunk.jpg" alt="Osiris surge tank with two Walbro 525 pumps, pressure gauge, braided AN lines and Fuel 1 / Fuel 2 relays mounted in a Genesis Coupe trunk" caption="Surge tank in the trunk with the pressure gauge and a relay for each pump." />
    <Figure src="fuel-line-bulkhead-fittings.jpg" alt="AN bulkhead fittings through the trunk floor of a Genesis Coupe for surge tank feed and return lines, viewed from under the car" caption="AN bulkhead fittings through the floor, seen from under the car." />
    <Figure src="surge-tank-trunk-mat.jpg" alt="Genesis Coupe trunk with the surge tank in the corner and the trunk mat back in place" caption="With the trunk mat back in, the tank sits in the corner and the trunk is still usable." />
  </div>

  <h2 id="fuel-pump-relay">Fuel Pump Relay</h2>
  <p>
    Upgraded fuel pumps should run a dedicated fuel pump power relay. I recommend watching Brian Paul's video on wiring his relay kit:
    <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BbU_6PtXyb0&t=814s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Genesis Coupe Fuel Pump Relay Kit | Install &amp; Overview</a>.
  </p>
  <p>The green and blue wires are fuel level — no need to touch them.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Wire</th><th>Goes to</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>OEM harness red/white (12V trigger)</td><td>Relay pin 86</td></tr>
      <tr><td>OEM harness black (ground)</td><td>Relay pin 85</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fuel pump black</td><td>Chassis ground (seat belt bolt is a good ground)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fuel pump red</td><td>Relay pin 87 (power to FP)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Red power wire from battery</td><td>Relay pin 30</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <Figure src="fuel-pump-relay-wiring.jpg" alt="Fuel pump relay wiring labeled battery to pin 30, harness ground to 85, trigger to 86, pin 87 to FP power" caption="Relay wiring: battery → 30, harness ground → 85, harness 12V trigger → 86, 87 → FP power." />
  <p>
    The red/white OEM fuel pump wire is also available at the passenger fuse box and near the driver's kick panel — see <a href="/guides/wiring#fuse-box">Wiring</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="evap">EVAP Solenoid</h2>
  <p>
    The current consensus is to remove the EVAP solenoid and leave the line next to the fuel feed line (where it comes into the engine bay) open,
    or go back to the carbon canister and unplug it there. Either way it needs to vent to atmosphere so the tank doesn't build pressure.
  </p>
  <Figure src="evap-solenoid-location.jpg" alt="EVAP line location circled in engine bay" caption="EVAP line next to the fuel feed." />

